(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-soap-case-presentations-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"SOAP Note and Case Presentations — Physical Diagnosis 1 Exam 3","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient describes her chest discomfort as “like something heavy sitting on me.” Which of the seven attributes of a symptom has she just given you?","choices":["Timing","Associated manifestations","Quality","Quantity or severity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Quality is the character of the symptom in the patient's own terms, such as sharp, dull, crushing or burning. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A patient rates her flank pain 6 out of 10. You then ask her what a 10 out of 10 would be for her. Why does the lecture want that question asked?","choices":["To check that the patient understands the scale in general","Because the number means little until the top of the scale is anchored","To record a baseline figure that is never reassessed later","To decide whether the pain is severe enough to justify imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture writes severity as “1-10, what is a 10?” for exactly this reason. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient says his back pain eases when he lies flat and worsens when he sits for long periods. Which two OPPQRST elements has he described?","choices":["Severity and timing","Palliative and provocative","Onset and timing","Quality and radiation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Palliative is what makes it better and provocative is what makes it worse. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"You are writing the subjective section for a patient with a three-day cough. She mentions in passing that her grandmother has arthritis. How does the lecture's guidance apply?","choices":["Leave it out, since family history never belongs in any focused note","Include it, since all family history counts as pertinent by default","Leave it out unless it bears on the complaint, a risk factor or a differential","Include it, because anything the patient volunteers must be documented"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture answers “what is pertinent?” with “it depends on the individual case” and gives those categories. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A patient presents with right-sided facial pain and nasal congestion. Applying the lecture's rule, how far should your examination reach?","choices":["Every system in full detail, so that nothing at all is missed","Only whatever can be examined without any special equipment","A system above and below the affected one, plus cardiac and respiratory","The nose alone, since that is where the complaint is centred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's rule when in doubt is to go a system up and down, and all examinations generally include cardiac and respiratory. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Before deciding what to examine, which question does the lecture want you to ask yourself?","choices":["Which findings are most likely to be normal?","What needs examining to rule the worst-case scenario in or out?","What will take the least time to document?","What did the last clinician examine at the previous visit?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The paired question is what your differentials are and how you can rule them in or out. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A student writes “Lungs: normal.” under the objective section. What does the lecture say is wrong with this?","choices":["The word normal is an abbreviation and should be spelled out","It never says what normal means for this particular patient","Lung findings belong in the assessment rather than the objective section","Nothing at all, provided the same wording is used consistently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks you to be specific, since normal and abnormal alone do not convey the finding. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"You list four possible diagnoses in the differential but write nothing after each one. What has the lecture asked for that is missing?","choices":["The name of the specialist who would go on to confirm each one","A numerical probability for each possibility","A brief statement tying the patient's findings to each possibility","A published literature citation supporting each possibility"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most likely comes first with supporting evidence; the others explain why they were considered and why they are less likely. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A colleague reads your differential and cannot tell why you favoured one diagnosis over the others. Which standard from the lecture has the note failed?","choices":["That the differential be limited to a single leading diagnosis","That the differential be written in the patient's own words","That other providers be able to understand your thought process from it","That every possible diagnosis for that organ be listed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this directly as the purpose of the differential statements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"You are treating a patient for a new sinus infection. She also has type 2 diabetes managed with metformin, which you have not mentioned anywhere in the note. What does the lecture require?","choices":["List the diabetes in the assessment, where chronic conditions belong","Record the diabetes only in the subjective, under past medical history","Nothing, since the diabetes is unrelated to the presenting complaint","Move the diabetes into the plan as an outpatient referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The assessment covers the final diagnosis plus other conditions requiring assessment or treatment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Your plan reads, in full, “Start an antibiotic.” Which required elements are missing?","choices":["The drug class and a second-line alternative","Indication, drug name, dosage, route, and frequency","Only the drug name, since the pharmacist selects the dose","The insurance authorisation number and pharmacy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires all five for a pharmacologic entry. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"You decide the patient can be managed at home rather than admitted. Under which element of the plan is that recorded?","choices":["Disposition","Testing","Referrals","Patient education"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Disposition is inpatient or outpatient, and the lecture lists it first among the plan's elements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which of the following belongs in the plan rather than anywhere else in the note?","choices":["The chronic conditions that also require treatment","A general assessment or overall impression of the patient","The seven attributes of the presenting symptom itself","Follow-up instructions, and what should bring the patient back sooner"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The plan covers disposition, testing, treatment, referrals, patient education and follow-up. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the components the lecture lists inside the subjective section?","choices":["Past medical and surgical history","Vital signs and measurements","Medications, allergies and reactions","The review of systems"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Measurements made during the encounter belong to the objective section. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the form the subjective section takes?","choices":["A checklist of positive and negative findings only","A table of measurements taken during the visit","A numbered list of the diagnoses being considered","A narrative of the history or state of symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is what the patient or their representative tells you, similar to the history of present illness. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A patient reports that his headache has been present every day for two weeks and never fully goes away. Which attribute does that describe?","choices":["Onset","Timing","Quality","Location"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Timing covers whether a symptom is intermittent or constant and how it changes over time. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In OLD ChARTS, what do the letters O and L stand for?","choices":["Onset and location","Origin and level","Onset and length","Occurrence and laterality"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. OLD ChARTS runs onset, location, duration, character, alleviating and aggravating factors, radiation, temporal pattern, and symptoms associated. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In ChLORIDEPP, what do the final two letters stand for?","choices":["Pattern and progression","Pain score and plan","Position and pressure","Palliative and provocative"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ChLORIDEPP runs character, location, onset, radiation, intensity, duration, extra symptoms, palliative, provocative. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which description matches the physical examination within the objective section?","choices":["Focused and detailed, covering the systems pertinent to the complaint","Limited strictly to the vital signs alone","Omitted entirely when the history is clear enough","A complete head-to-toe examination in every single case"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits alongside observations, measurements and tests performed during the encounter. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Besides explaining why each possibility was considered, what else may the differential include, according to the lecture?","choices":["The tests that would help rule it in or out","The likely length of any hospital stay for each","The likely cost of treating each possibility","The patient's own stated preference among them"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture allows the differential to name those tests. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which pair of elements does the lecture list under treatment within the plan?","choices":["Inpatient and outpatient care","Urgent and routine referral","Pharmacologic and non-pharmacologic","Diagnostic and therapeutic testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both sit under treatment, alongside disposition, testing, referrals, education and follow-up. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does the lecture define an oral case presentation?","choices":["A well organised vignette describing the patient and the problem","A summary limited to the final diagnosis and the medication prescribed","A discussion of the published literature supporting the diagnosis","A verbatim reading of the written note to the supervising clinician on duty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It helps listeners visualise the patient and understand the problem. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which situation does the lecture list as an occasion for making a presentation?","choices":["Presenting to consulting practitioners","Teaching a formal seminar to a group of students","Documenting the encounter in the electronic record","Explaining the diagnosis to the patient's family at the bedside"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists preceptors and supervising physicians, consulting practitioners, rounds, and phone calls. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which four things does the lecture say an oral presentation conveys?","choices":["The patient's demographics, insurance, referral source, and appointment record","The diagnosis, the prognosis, the cost, and the expected total length of stay","The history, the examination, the literature, and the published guidelines","What the patient told you, what you found, what you concluded, and what you plan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only information relevant to the patient's condition is included. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"In what order does the lecture say an oral presentation should run?","choices":["Beginning with the assessment, so the listener knows the conclusion first","In the order the information was obtained during the encounter","Beginning with the physical examination findings","The same order as written reports"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That order runs from chief complaint and history of present illness through to assessment and plan. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"You are presenting a patient you are seeing for the first time. How much does the lecture say to include?","choices":["Only the medications and allergies","Only what has changed since the last evaluation","The chief complaint and active problems only","The complete medical history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. New patients get the complete history; established patients in clinic get the chief complaint and active problems. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"You are presenting an inpatient you also saw yesterday. What does the lecture say a follow-up presentation covers?","choices":["The status of active problems, new concerns, and any changes","Only the results of any tests that returned since yesterday","The complete medical history again, for completeness","Only the medications that were given overnight"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives all three for follow-up visits, inpatient or outpatient. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which pearl does the lecture offer for delivering a presentation?","choices":["Present the findings in whatever order they happen to come to mind","Tell a story, and try to make it interesting","Include every detail, since listeners cannot ask questions afterwards","Read directly from your notes so that nothing at all is omitted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pearls are to stay relaxed, keep order, avoid reading notes, tell an interesting story, and err on saying less. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the lecture's KISS principle stand for, and what does it ask of a note?","choices":["Keep Information Sequential and Signed — chronological order only","Keep It Structured and Standardised — identical wording each visit","Keep It Simple Students — logical progression, headings, specificity","Keep It Short and Systematic — one page for each encounter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs it with head-to-toe progression, appropriate headings, being specific, and avoiding redundancy. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the lecture's rule on abbreviations, and why?","choices":["Use only abbreviations approved by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services","Use them freely, provided a glossary is attached","Use them only in the assessment and plan","Avoid them — when in doubt, spell it out — because they cause misunderstandings and errors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The closing summary asks that another provider always be able to understand exactly what you were told, saw, thought, decided and said. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
